# How a pull request is tested

A pull request (PR) is a developer's package of changes waiting to be released. Use this file every
time PRs are handed over for testing on {{main_env}}.

## 1. What's in scope

1. **Every screen PR on the list**, tested through the screens the way a user sees them.
2. **The server PRs behind each screen PR**, judged by what the screens show: prices, charges,
   saves, limits, messages, admin settings. Server behaviour with no screen goes in a separate
   "server-only" list, not in the issues.
3. **Admin changes** the PR causes: screens, dialogs and real saves. Put shared settings back after.
4. **Impact map, built from the diff, not guessed.** List every shared piece the PR touches —
   components, hooks, email templates, server routes, message/translation keys, feature flags — then
   find **every place that uses each one**, on every portal{{#if has_mobile}} and the {{mobile.platforms}} app{{/if}},
   not only the screens the PR mentions. Each shared piece gets a row in `COVERAGE.md`: what it is,
   who else uses it, checked?, result{{#if sources.code_path}}
   (the code is at `{{sources.code_path}}`: `git -C {{sources.code_path}} fetch` then `git -C {{sources.code_path}} diff <base>...origin/<branch>`, read only){{/if}}.
5. **Every account state the change can reach**, for every role.
6. **Start with a coverage grid** (`runs/<date>-<name>/COVERAGE.md`): PR × server PRs × roles ×
   states × design frames × shared-piece impact × gaps. Create the run folder with `npm run new-run -- <name>`.
7. **Check `docs/qa/PRIOR-GAPS.md` first.** Anything left "Not covered" or deferred by an earlier run,
   on a surface this PR touches, gets tested now. Update that file when the run ends.
{{#if has_security}}
8. **Security checks on anything new**: a new field, a new screen, a new role boundary or a new
   endpoint gets the checks in `docs/qa/rules/SECURITY-RULES.md`, not only the happy path.
{{/if}}
{{#if has_db}}
9. **Confirm a save against the database** for this PR's main write, per `docs/qa/rules/DB-RULES.md` —
   read-only, never to create a state.
{{/if}}

Account states for this project: {{states}}.
Roles: {{#each roles}}{{name}}{{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}.

## 2. Design check, frame by frame

{{#unless has_designs}}
_No design files are linked yet. Until they are, compare each screen with the previous build and
the spec, and list "design check" as Not covered in the report._

{{/unless}}
- Check only the **newest** designs, and only the frames for the screens and states **this PR** builds or changes.
- Only the portal the PR is for. Other pages the PR touches are compared with the previous build for side effects.
- For each frame: text, order, states (default, hover, disabled, empty, error), sizes, spacing, fonts,
  colours, corners, shadows, icons. An icon is checked against the design's icon **component**, not
  just "looks close enough" — and the same icon is compared on every portal that shows it, not only
  the one the PR names.
- **Read values, don't guess them.** Design values come from the design file (Figma connector).
  Live values come from the page (element size and computed style). Never measure screenshots.
- Re-list the design frames every run. Frame ids change.
- **Inventory before judging.** List every page → section → frame the PR touches (every state: default,
  hover, disabled, empty, error, every modal) before giving any verdict. A screen with no listed frame
  is "Not covered: no design for this screen", never skipped silently.
- Differences of {{rules.min_design_diff_px}}px or less, or near-identical shades, stay out.
- Write results to `DESIGN.md` in the run folder: frame | screen | match? | values | proof.
- Problems that existed before the PR, on a page this PR does not touch, are reported in their own
  "already there before" group, not dropped — unless they are both very minor (polish only, not
  money, access, data or sign-in) and provably untouched by this change: then leave them out and say
  so in the coverage grid.

Design files:
{{#each sources.designs}}
- {{name}}: {{url}}
{{/each}}

## 3. Emails and messages

{{#if sends_email}}
Every action that can send mail is checked. See `EMAIL-CHECKS.md`. Keep a log per lane in `EMAILS.md`.
{{else}}
Every message the change shows or sends (notices{{#if has_mobile}}, push notifications{{/if}}) is checked: once, right people, right text, working links.
{{/if}}

## 4. Test accounts, payments and settings

{{#if only_production}}
- **There is no test environment.** Nothing is created, paid or changed; flows that need it are "Not covered".
{{/if}}
- Test accounts may be approved and charged on test environments{{#if has_payments}} ({{payments.provider}} test mode, {{payments.test_details}}){{/if}}. Record every charge against the expected amount.
{{#if has_levers}}
- Settings that change behaviour ({{levers}}): test the change under each value it can meet, and put shared values back.
{{/if}}
- Admin switches may be used to reach a state. **Put every shared value back** and write it in `RESTORE-LOG.md`.
- Changing admin permissions on real staff accounts always needs the lead's OK first.
{{#if production}}
- Never touch `{{production.name}}` beyond read-only smoke checks.
{{/if}}

## 5. Tested from the screen

Every claim is judged by what the user sees, in a fresh headless sign-in. Two exceptions, always labelled:

1. **Instrumented checks.** States the screens can't reach on a shared environment (slow server,
   loading skeletons, a server error, a missing field) may be forced with Playwright `route`. Label the
   result `instrumented`. It can confirm a **pass** only. It becomes an issue only if the same failure
   also happens from the screens alone, {{rules.recheck_times}} times.
2. **Setup through admin tools or the API.** Fine for reaching a state for a pass. If anything fails in
   that state, set it up again through the real screens before it goes to the re-checker.

## 6. The use-case gate

Before a PR report is shared, build `USE-CASES.md` in the run folder from the PR itself:

- every user story, acceptance criterion and requirement in the PR and its spec
- each role, including read-only ones; each account state; each portal the PR touches
{{#if sends_email}}
- each email the actions send
{{/if}}
{{#if has_mobile}}
- the same change in the {{mobile.platforms}} app, if the app uses it
{{/if}}
{{#if has_locales}}
- each language ({{locales_text}})
{{/if}}
- edge cases: double click, two tabs saving at once, empty / too long / wrong input, slow or no
  network, links and redirects, opening a page by address without permission

Each row gets one status: **Pass** · **Fail → issue id** · **Not covered + reason**.
A blank row means the run isn't finished.

**Not covered stays near zero.** Before writing it, try to unblock yourself first: create the
fixture, reach the state, make the account you need on {{main_env}}. "Not covered" survives only
when it's truly impossible on this environment, or doing it would break another lane's run in
progress — and the row says which, and what you tried. "No time" is never a reason.

## 7. The report

Groups, in this order:

1. Caused by this PR
2. Already there before, on the pages it touches
3. Design differences
4. Questions for the product owner (only after `PO-QUESTION-RULE.md`)
5. Server-only notes
6. The use-case checklist with the "Not covered" list, the coverage grid and the restore log

Format: {{rules.report}}. Every issue follows `TICKET-RULES.md` and has passed the re-check.

## 8. Publishing the report

1. `qa-reporter` writes `REPORT-DRAFT.md`. `qa-rechecker` re-checks every candidate.
2. **Audit before publishing.** A fresh pass (the lead, or an agent that didn't work this run) checks
   the whole run folder against `docs/qa/rules/RULES-CHECKLIST.md`. A gap sends it back to the owning
   lane, not into the report.
3. The lead writes `REPORT.md` in the run folder (its template is already there), with **only** the
   re-checked issues, the use-case table and the Not covered list. Screenshots are linked from the
   run folder: `![what it shows](lane/03-step.png)`.
   **Open items only.** Leave out anything already Fixed, Ignored or ruled an accepted behaviour, and
   leave out run history (who found what, which try). A reader should see only what still needs
   action. Move anything left "Not covered" or deferred into `docs/qa/PRIOR-GAPS.md` before sharing.
4. `npm run report` turns it into `REPORT.html`: one page, screenshots inside. It refuses if a proof
   file is missing or a password, token or card number is in the text.
{{#if report_artifact}}
5. **Publish `REPORT.html` as a Claude artifact** ("publish runs/<run>/REPORT.html as an artifact").
   Artifacts start private: the lead shares the link with the developers. Save the link in
   `REPORT-LINK.txt`.
6. **Retests and updates go to the same link.** Publish the updated page again from the same file, so
   everyone keeps one address. Each time, remove what's now fixed or ignored first — the page always
   shows only what's still open, not a history of what used to be wrong.
7. No artifacts in your Claude Code (they need the Claude desktop app or claude.ai)? Share `REPORT.html`
   itself: it opens in any browser, offline.
{{else}}
{{#if report_tracker}}
5. The lead files each issue in {{#if sources.tracker}}{{sources.tracker}}{{else}}the tracker{{/if}} from `REPORT.md`, and shares `REPORT.html` as the summary.
{{else}}
5. Share `REPORT.md` (or `REPORT.html`, which opens in any browser) with the developers.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}
